{
  "result": "On some mornings, the world seems to wake up reluctantly. The street is still, the windows across the way are dark, and even the birds sound as if they are testing their voices before committing to a song. A kettle clicks on. Somewhere, a door closes softly. The day has begun, but it has not yet made any demands.\n\nI like to think of this hour as a small public park in time. Anyone can visit, though few people stay for long. There are no tickets, no gates, and no particular reason to be there. You can sit with a cup of tea, watch the light gather on a wall, and let your thoughts wander without asking them to become useful.\n\nLater, the familiar machinery starts: messages arrive, buses hiss at their stops, and lists grow longer even as items are crossed off. None of this is necessarily bad. Work can be satisfying, noise can be cheerful, and a crowded afternoon can carry its own kind of music. Still, it helps to remember that the quieter hour existed first.\n\nIt does not solve anything. The unwashed dishes remain, the difficult conversation still waits, and tomorrow will bring fresh complications. But that brief interval offers a different measure of a life: not only what gets accomplished, but what gets noticed. A patch of gold on the floor. Steam rising from a mug. The astonishing fact that, for a moment, there is enough time to see them before the whole world stirs.",
